evidence_text:
1) UnpackContent sets FlowFile filename/path attributes directly from the raw archive entry name (no normalization/isolation):
   - [/nifi-nar-bundles/nifi-standard-bundle/nifi-standard-processors/src/main/java/org/apache/nifi/processors/standard/UnpackContent.java:444-452] shows the code constructing a File from the zip entry name and placing file.getName() into CoreAttributes.FILENAME and file.getParent() into CoreAttributes.PATH:
       final File file = new File(zipEntryName);
       final String parentDirectory = (file.getParent() == null) ? PATH_SEPARATOR : file.getParent();
       attributes.put(CoreAttributes.FILENAME.key(), file.getName());
       attributes.put(CoreAttributes.PATH.key(), parentDirectory);
   - Because zipEntryName is taken from the archive entry (ZipArchiveEntry.getName() / LocalFileHeader.getFileName()), an attacker-controlled archive entry such as "../../etc/passwd" will cause PATH = "../../.." and FILENAME = "passwd".

2) PutFile allows using FlowFile attributes in the Directory property and resolves the configured path without containing canonicalization or allowlist checks:
   - [/nifi-nar-bundles/nifi-standard-bundle/nifi-standard-processors/src/main/java/org/apache/nifi/processors/standard/PutFile.java:112-118] documents the DIRECTORY property and explicitly gives an example using ${path} (expression language supported from FlowFile attributes):
       .description("The directory to which files should be written. You may use expression language such as /aa/bb/${path}")
       .expressionLanguageSupported(ExpressionLanguageScope.FLOWFILE_ATTRIBUTES)
   - [/nifi-nar-bundles/nifi-standard-bundle/nifi-standard-processors/src/main/java/org/apache/nifi/processors/standard/PutFile.java:225-236] shows the code evaluating the Directory property against FlowFile attributes and resolving paths:
       final Path configuredRootDirPath = Paths.get(context.getProperty(DIRECTORY).evaluateAttributeExpressions(flowFile).getValue());
       final Path rootDirPath = configuredRootDirPath.toAbsolutePath();
       String filename = flowFile.getAttribute(CoreAttributes.FILENAME.key());
       final Path tempCopyFile = rootDirPath.resolve("." + filename);
       final Path copyFile = rootDirPath.resolve(filename);
   - There is no subsequent call to normalize()/toRealPath() followed by a containment check (e.g., verifying that copyFile startsWith(someBaseCanonicalPath)), nor any sanitization of '..' path elements coming from attributes.

3) Practical dataflow and attacker model (how attacker control is realistic):
   - UnpackContent is an unpacking processor that reads archive entries from FlowFiles and sets PATH/FILENAME attributes derived from entry names (evidence above). FlowFiles commonly arrive from network-facing sources (HTTP, FTP, etc.) in NiFi flows — NiFi is designed to accept external data into flows and processors like UnpackContent are used to process uploaded archives. The PutFile processor writes FlowFiles to the local filesystem and explicitly supports using FlowFile attributes in the DIRECTORY configuration (evidence above).
   - Therefore, an attacker able to send an archive to a NiFi flow that applies UnpackContent and then PutFile (configured with DIRECTORY using ${path} or similar) can craft archive entry names with path traversal elements (../ or absolute paths) to influence the evaluated directory and cause writes outside the intended directory.

Conclusion: The code in this repository contains a runtime-reachable pattern that meets the CWE-22 criteria (attacker-influenced path components derived from archive entries are used to construct filesystem paths for writing, without robust canonicalization/containment checks). The test therefore FAILs.

If you need, I can (a) search for any mitigations or unit tests in the repo that specifically sanitize path components for UnpackContent+PutFile usage, or (b) produce a concrete PoC flow (sequence of processors and properties) showing the exact attribute values and resulting filesystem path that would be written.
